<text>
Reason:
<text>

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
if (WARN_ON_ONCE(!test_and_clear_bit(GD_SUPPRESS_PART_SCAN,
				     &ub->ub_disk->state)))
    return;
mutex_lock(&ub->ub_disk->open_mutex);
bdev_disk_changed(ub->ub_disk, false);
mutex_unlock(&ub->ub_disk->open_mutex);
```
and
```
flush_work(&ub->partition_scan_work);    // in ublk_stop_dev()
```
Reason:  
The worker `ublk_partition_scan_work` directly dereferences `ub->ub_disk` without holding a reference, so if `ublk_stop_dev` runs concurrently (calling `del_gendisk`, setting `ub->ub_disk = NULL` via `ublk_detach_disk`, and `put_disk` freeing the disk), the worker's subsequent access to `ub->ub_disk->state` or `ub->ub_disk->open_mutex` results in use-after-free. Additionally, `flush_work` only waits for the work to complete but does not prevent it from starting after the disk is already detached, while `cancel_work_sync` would have avoided scheduling the work at that point.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
struct gendisk *disk = ublk_get_disk(ub);
if (!disk)
    return;
...
out:
    ublk_put_disk(disk);
```
and
```
cancel_work_sync(&ub->partition_scan_work);    // replaces flush_work()
```
Reason:  
`ublk_get_disk()` acquires a reference to the disk while holding a spinlock that synchronizes with `ublk_detach_disk()`, ensuring the worker either gets a valid `disk` pointer with an elevated refcount or sees `NULL` and exits early. This prevents use-after-free because the disk cannot be freed while the worker holds the reference. Changing `flush_work` to `cancel_work_sync` ensures that if the work hasn't started yet, it will be cancelled instead of allowed to run after teardown.

